  "query_preview": "..Over the past couple of mornings we have seen higher \nrelative humidity lingering around resulting in some patchy fog over \nour eastern areas. We still could see patchy fog over the next \nmorning, but overall we're seeing a gradual drying trend. Otherwise, \na beautiful day today with highs topping out in the 70s for most \nvalley locations. As the next system approaches Friday we'll see an \nuptick in afternoon breeziness east and south of Tucson with \nlocalized wind gusts of around 20 to 25 mph. Friday night into \nSaturday we'll have a slight chance (5-10%) for light rain/snow \nshowers in the White Mountains, but overall the trough has trended \nfurther north along with its forcing for precipitation chances.\n\nSaturday we will dry out substantially enough that by Sunday \nmorning we could see low temperatures across our eastern valley \nlocations possibly approach or dip below freezing resulting in \npatchy frost and localized freezing conditions for a few hours. \nLatest NBM probabilities for low temperatures below 32 degrees \nare between 20 to 40 percent on Sunday and again on Tuesday \nmornings across the San Pedro Valley and Sulphur Springs Valley.\nFreezing temperatures could damage vulnerable plants and crops.\nOtherwise, high temperatures will remain near normal with day \nto day variability and slim chances for a light rain/snow shower\nor two again in the White Mountains on Monday/Tuesday timeframe\nassociated with shortwave trough.",
